--TEST--
`Core\Server::isDraining` answers a program that no accept loop is draining, rather than refusing a question about a server that is not there — ADR 0097 § 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0097 § 5's last sentence hands an application the fact `[server]
// health_path` answers a proxy with, so that an endpoint of its own can report
// the same shutdown. A CLI program is the case with no server at all, and the
// answer here is the boundary: `false`, not a throw and not a refusal, because
// nothing has begun a shutdown and that is a thing this program may be told.
//
// The member reads one process-wide bit that the accept loop is the only
// writer of — `crates/nvs-runtime/src/drain.rs` owns it, and `nvs-stdlib`
// reads it there rather than from `nvs-server`, which it does not depend on. A
// member that instead read a *server's* own state would have nothing to read
// on this line at all.
if (Core\Server::isDraining()) {
    echo "draining\n";
} else {
    echo "accepting\n";
}

// Asked with no `nvs.toml` and no grant: the drain is a fact about the process
// rather than a door, so nothing has to be configured or granted for the
// question to have an answer. A case that needed either would be testing the
// configuration and not this member.
echo "asked with nothing configured\n";
--EXPECT--
accepting
asked with nothing configured
